The tracking of the forklift consists of an outer and inner tracking module. The inner tracking module estimates the position and orientation (pose) of the mast using two sensors, a tilt sensor and a string potentiometer. The outer tracking module determines the pose of the forklift relative to the world origin by using two rotary encoders.

We implement the algorithms on a desktop computer located on the forklift using the game engine Unity. The real-time communication with the HMD (Microsoft HoloLens 2) operates via the holographic remoting player. The HMD displays the rendered frame and sends the head pose data to the registration module. All of the tracking data is utilized to render a scene captured by the five RGB-D cameras. Fig. 5 shows the different transformations in our system. For the accurate visualization, we transfer all coordinate systems into one common origin. Here, some transformations are dynamic, i.e. they change at runtime of the program, such as the transformation FL T AR between the HMD and the forklift. Other transformations, such as the relationship between the lift mast cameras MC T ML and MC T MR are determined initially and are static with respect to each other.
