The results show an oscillation around a mean value. The oscillations around the mean value are significantly larger for the y-position. The y-position also shows some outliers. In general, there is no drift of the measured values in the investigated time period. To investigate the accuracy and precision, we investigate several scene distances between the Hololens and the forklift. Furthermore, we investigate the impact of the frame rate and resolution for the scene reconstruction. For the validation, we apply ISO 9283 [14] . The standard distinguishes between accuracy and a repeatability (precision). Unlike in ISO 9283, we do not perform spatial measurements in this study, as we determine the measurement error in 2D augmentation ( Fig. 7 ). The two dimensional accuracy A x y with the mean values x ¯ and y ¯ of the detected upper left marker corner points x i , y i in frame i is defined as: (1) A x y = ( x ¯ − x E P ) 2 + ( y ¯ − y E P ) 2 (2) x ¯ = 1 n ∑ i = 1 n x i ; = 1 n ∑ i = 1 n y i

The accuracy describes the distance between the set point we calculate by the linear extrapolation ( x E P , y E P ) and the center of gravity of the measured values. A normal distribution is assumed. The 3 σ -precision P x y describes the environment around the expected value, where we find 99.73% of all measured values and is defined as: (3) P x y = l ¯ + 3 σ (4) l ¯ = 1 n ∑ i = 1 n l i (5) l i = ( x i − x ¯ ) 2 + ( y i − y ¯ ) 2 (6) σ = ∑ i = 1 n ( l i − l ¯ ) n − 1 with the distance of each detected marker l i to the mean value x ¯ in frame i , the mean value of the deviations l ¯ and the standard deviation σ . Fig. 9 shows the results for accuracy and precision for different distances between fork tip and pattern.
