From Fig. 9 it is obvious that the accuracy increases with increasing working distance. As a result, the deviation between reality and virtuality increases. At a distance of 2 m, the smallest measured deviation is 43.5 px, at 3 m 74.9 px and at 4 m 95.6 px. In general, there is no significant difference between the camera parameters considered in the near and far range. The jitter of the measured values (precision) decreases with increasing distance. At a distance of 2 m, the precision is 5.3 px, at 3 m 3.8 px and at 4 m 2.8 px. When using forklifts in indoor locations at maximum speed of v max  = 20 km/h, a maximum deviation of 300 mm in a distance of 10 m should be achieved. The experimental results show that we even have a deviation of 98 px at a distance of 4 m in front of the forklift, which corresponds to a deviation of 297 mm in the real world.
3.2. Temporal error

Latencies in DR systems lead to different error patterns. Fig. 10 shows a simplified representation of latencies in our system. The sensors used for tracking and scene recording are not synchronized. Accordingly, relative latencies between the incoming data streams D i (e.g. camera streams, head tracking data) occur during the data acquisition [15] . This leads to different end-to-end latencies for each sensor.
